---
/**
 * How to reach the person who maintains this.
 *
 * There is no form here and there cannot be: the site is static files on a
 * CDN with no server to receive a POST, which is the same fact `/privacy/`
 * states from the other direction. So this page routes to the channels that
 * do exist and says what a useful report contains, which is the part that
 * actually decides whether a correction gets made.
 */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { buildInfo } from "../build-info";
import { pageHref } from "../nav";
import { AUTHOR, AUTHOR_EMAIL } from "../seo";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const { zigVersion } = await buildInfo();
---

<Base
  title="Contact"
  seoTitle="Contact the maintainer of Zig Guide Live"
  description="How to report an error in a chapter, suggest a topic, or reach Rajesh Pillai about this Zig guide."
  crumbs={[{ label: "Contact" }]}
  schema={{
    "@type": "ContactPage",
    name: "Contact",
    mainEntity: { "@type": "Person", name: AUTHOR, email: `mailto:${AUTHOR_EMAIL}` },
  }}
>
  <article>
    <h1>Contact</h1>

    <p class="lede">
      Rajesh Pillai writes and maintains this guide. Corrections, questions
      and requests all go to the same place, and he reads all of them.
    </p>

    <p>
      To contact him directly, send an email to{" "}
      <a href={`mailto:${AUTHOR_EMAIL}`}>{AUTHOR_EMAIL}</a>. The sections below
      tell you which channel gives the fastest fix for each type of problem.
    </p>

    <h2>Something on a page is wrong</h2>
    <p>
      This report is the most useful thing that you can send. Open an issue
      on{" "}
      <a
        href="https://github.com/rajeshpillai/zig-guide/issues"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer">the issue tracker</a>. If a report has these
      four items, the author can usually act on it immediately:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>The URL of the chapter.</li>
      <li>
        Your Zig version, from <code>zig version</code>. The guide targets
        master, and CI last verified it against {zigVersion}. In about half of
        the reports, a tagged release runs code that was written for master.
        The chapter{" "}
        <a href={pageHref("getting-started/coming-from-older-zig")}
          >Migrate from an Older Zig</a> is about that problem.
      </li>
      <li>The result that you expected.</li>
      <li>The result that you got, with the full error message.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      If a snippet does not compile for you on current master, the nightly
      build will soon fail on it. Please report it immediately. CI compiles and
      runs every snippet each night, so this problem should not be possible. If
      it occurs, then the check also has a problem. To learn how the check
      works, read{" "}
      <a href={`${base}verification/`}>how this guide is verified</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>A chapter is missing, or one is unclear</h2>
    <p>
      Use the issue tracker for this too. Say what you tried to do, and where
      you could not continue. "I could not find how to do X" is more useful
      than a proposed table of contents. The real gap is usually not where it
      seems to be.
    </p>

    <h2>A correction that you wrote</h2>
    <p>
      You can send pull requests to{" "}
      <a
        href="https://github.com/rajeshpillai/zig-guide"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer">github.com/rajeshpillai/zig-guide</a>. Every
      snippet is a real file that the build compiles and runs. Thus, when you
      push a change to example code, CI checks it automatically. The notes in
      the repository tell how the build classifies snippets, and what a new
      snippet needs.
    </p>

    <h2>Anything else</h2>
    <p>
      For a message that does not belong in a public issue, write to{" "}
      <a href={`mailto:${AUTHOR_EMAIL}`}>{AUTHOR_EMAIL}</a>. Rajesh is also on X
      as{" "}
      <a href="https://twitter.com/rajeshpillai" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >@rajeshpillai</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>What this site cannot do</h2>
    <p>
      This site has no accounts, no comment system and no server. Nothing here
      can receive a form or store a message. Thus every route above goes to a
      different domain. The address at the top goes to a mailbox, not to a
      program on this site. For the same reason, the{" "}
      <a href={`${base}privacy/`}>privacy</a> page is short.
    </p>

    <p><a href={`${base}about/`}>About this guide →</a></p>
  </article>
</Base>
